<div class="meta">Lesson 0011 · Concurrency · from fix(llm): take the queue mutex before signalling worker shutdown</div>
<h1>Condition Variables and the Lost Wakeup</h1>
<p>A real bug from this codebase: the game's test suite hung roughly one run in
five, and the fix was two lines of lock scope.</p>

<h2>The crime scene</h2>
<p><code>LlmClient</code> runs one worker thread that sleeps on a condition variable until
there's a request (or shutdown). The destructor looked reasonable:</p>
<pre><code>// BUGGY — as it was before the fix
LlmClient::~LlmClient() {
    stop_.store(true);          // stop_ is a std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;
    requestCv_.notify_all();
    if (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();   // hung here, forever
}

// worker side
std::unique_lock&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(requestMutex_);
requestCv_.wait(lock, [this] { return stop_.load() || !requests_.empty(); });</code></pre>
<p><code>stop_</code> is atomic, so no data race. <code>notify_all()</code> is called after the store.
What could possibly go wrong?</p>

<h2>The interleaving that kills it</h2>
<pre><code>worker thread                          destructor thread
─────────────────────────────          ─────────────────────────────
locks requestMutex_
evaluates predicate → false
                                       stop_.store(true)
                                       requestCv_.notify_all()   ← nobody is waiting yet!
blocks on the CV (releasing mutex)
...sleeps forever — the notify already happened</code></pre>
<p><code>wait(lock, pred)</code> is a loop: check predicate → if false, atomically
release-and-block. The atomicity protects against wakeups <em>while blocking</em> —
but there is a gap between <em>evaluating the predicate</em> and <em>starting to block</em>.
A notification delivered in that gap hits no waiter and vanishes. That is the
<strong>lost wakeup</strong>. The worker then sleeps waiting for a notify that will never
come again, and <code>join()</code> waits for the worker: deadlock.</p>
<div class="callout warn">Atomics are the trap here. Because <code>stop_</code> is atomic, the code
compiles cleanly, passes TSan's data-race checks, and works 99% of the time.
Atomicity of the flag does <em>nothing</em> to order the flag-write against the
waiter's evaluate-then-block sequence.</div>

<h2>The fix: write the predicate state under the mutex</h2>
<pre><code>LlmClient::~LlmClient() {
    {
        std::lock_guard&lt;std::mutex&gt; lock(requestMutex_);
        stop_.store(true);              // now serialized with the wait
    }
    requestCv_.notify_all();
    if (worker_.joinable()) worker_.join();
}</code></pre>
<p>Why does holding the mutex close the gap? The worker evaluates its predicate
<em>while holding <code>requestMutex_</code></em>. If the destructor must also hold that mutex to
flip <code>stop_</code>, only two orderings exist:</p>
<ol>
<li>Destructor gets the mutex <em>first</em> → worker's predicate check happens after,
sees <code>stop_ == true</code>, never blocks.</li>
<li>Worker gets the mutex first → it is <em>fully blocked</em> on the CV (the
release-and-block is atomic) before the destructor can take the mutex —
so the notify finds a waiter.</li>
</ol>
<p>The gap is gone by construction. Note that <code>notify_all()</code> itself may stay
outside the lock — it's the <em>predicate state change</em> that must be inside.</p>

<h2>The rule to remember</h2>
<div class="callout good"><strong>Any write that makes a condition-variable predicate become true
must happen while holding the same mutex the waiter uses</strong> — even if the write is
atomic. (Corollary: <code>enqueue()</code> in this same file was already correct: it pushes
to the queue under the mutex, then notifies.)</div>

<h2>How it was caught, and how it's kept dead</h2>
<p>Multiplayer's loopback tests run the whole suite more often and construct more
threaded objects; a routine 5×-run flake check hung on run 3. <code>sample &lt;pid&gt;</code>
on the stuck process showed one thread in <code>~LlmClient → join()</code> and the worker in
<code>condition_variable::wait</code> — the two-line stack trace <em>is</em> the diagnosis.</p>
<p>The regression test exploits the race window directly: constructing and
immediately destroying <code>LlmClient</code> 200 times hits the
destructor-vs-wait-entry timing over and over. Pre-fix it hangs the suite within
a few runs; post-fix it's deterministic. You can't unit-test a race into
impossibility, but you can make the odds of missing it astronomically small.</p>
